+\subsection{The public running condition}
+The last estimate is the input \eqref{eq:running} of
+Section~\ref{sec:running-coefficient} for the selected histories, read as
+follows. Write $\beta_{N,p,m}$ for the coupling at level $m\le N$ of the
+selected history at cutoff $N$ and period index $p$. Its scale is
+$s_m=S_m=\log(a_m^{-1})$, where $a_m=e^{-S_m}$ is the reciprocal of the
+integer product of the first $m$ block sides, and $S_m\to\infty$. Its
+intercept is $\bar\beta$, and $2b_0(G)=s$ by \eqref{eq:shoot-slope}. For
+every $\eta>0$ the preceding paragraph supplies a level after which
+$|\beta_{N,p,m}-(\bar\beta+2b_0(G)S_m)|\le\eta S_m$ for every $N\ge m$ and
+every $p$. When the coarse target $b$ of the descent equals $\bar\beta$,
+as in the selected run, the term $|b-\bar\beta|$ in
+\eqref{eq:shoot-running-error} vanishes. It is in any case a constant, and
+was absorbed above.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Public running condition]
+\label{prop:shoot-public-running}
+Let the public family have the selected histories as its runs: at cutoff $N$
+and period index $p$, its lattice has $P_pe^{S_N}$ sites per axis, so that
+$D_N=e^{S_N}$ and $a_N=D_N^{-1}$, and its bare coefficient $\beta_{N,p}$
+is the coupling $\beta_{N,p,N}$ of the bare Wilson density with which the
+selected history at cutoff $N$ starts. Then \eqref{ex-running-input} holds
+with $\sigma=s_G=2b_0(G)$.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:uniform-extraction} with $A=\bar\beta$,
+$c=2b_0(G)$ and $s_m=S_m$; its hypothesis is the estimate just recorded.
+Given $\varepsilon>0$, it gives $N_0$ such that, for every $m\ge N_0$,
+$S_m>0$ and $|\beta_{N,p,m}/S_m-c|<\varepsilon$ for all $N\ge m$ and all
+$p$. Take $m=N$. For $N\ge N_0$ and every $p$, the identities
+$\beta_{N,p,N}=\beta_{N,p}$ and $S_N=\log(D_N)=\log(a_N^{-1})$ give
+\[
+ \left|\frac{\beta_{N,p}}{\log(a_N^{-1})}-c\right|<\varepsilon .
+\]
+The threshold $N_0$ does not depend on $p$. Finally $c=2b_0(G)>0$, so
+$\sigma=c$ satisfies \eqref{ex-running-input}.
+\end{proof}
+
+The proposition uses only the uniformity in $N\ge m$ and $p$ of the running
+estimate, at the diagonal $m=N$. It does not use the convergence of the
+histories as the cutoff is removed.
